    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "So one thing to understand is obviously having a democratically controlled house. your bill doesn't get to the floor if you don't have a bipartisan co-sponsor. If you're a Democrat, your bill has a lower chance of getting introduced if it doesn't have a bipartisan co-sponsor. I serve on the Homeland Security Foreign Affairs and Science Base and Technology Committees. of those are reflexively partisan. You don't have traditional liberal versus conservative issues, there may be some quibbling in disagreement kind of on the margins on the extent of engagement in one place or the dynamics of action in another. But by and large, I serve in areas where there's broad agreement and where the issues that are being put forth often are often many of the bills I co-sponsor. They just go on the suspension calendar. They pass out a committee unanimously. There's not a ton of dissension on whether or not to condemn China's treatment of the weaker population. That's going to get overwhelming support because it's just acknowledging the truth. So I was, I didn't intend to be, well, I obviously wanted to be productive, and then I think in order to be productive, it helps to engage in a bipartisan and trusted way. A lot of that score is not bipartisanship for, you know, appearance is sake, but because we're working on issues of legislation, whether it's on dealing with veterans, whether it's dealing with military or national security, that just lend",
