    "turn_text": "you ask me the question is there a problem with incentives i mean the obvious answers yes uh... remember One colleague who was being stripped of committees, she called a press conference. I think we had a competing press conference. One or two cameras showed up when we're talking about authorizations for use of military force, right? Or things that are actually tangibly important and deal with whether or not the president can send men and women to die in the name of the country. These are lofty, weighty issues that would barely get any intention. Meanwhile, you know, the camera kind of flocks to, you know, the political car accident, right, to kind of glock and stare. It makes sense that things are going to trend towards the extremes, right? You, people to paraphrase Dimitri Martin, nobody spray paints. Toy Story 2 is okay. It's going to be either, it's going to be love or hate and a lot of what Congress does is or should be a little bit boring. It may have very impactful outcomes, but it doesn't lend itself to the people who are doing it are evil on this side and the ones who are trying to stop it. There is a lot more gray in many of those issues than I think our media and our entertainment cycle really amplifying. So the more that we can get away from simplistic narratives, the far better off we can be, but the challenge is, I mean, we view our world through narrative constructs, and that's just part of the human condition. So it'll inherently be a balancing act between who we are as individuals and what this is. But it's certainly the case that if you're looking to find meeting and entertainment in politics, the politics becomes the worst when it seeks to offer both of those.",
