    "turn_text": "least surprising was just how the incentive is not geared towards that if you just kind of go with the flow. And we had some very difficult votes early on. I would say that the least surprising was that there were folks who just didn't care. Not a lot of them, but there's an argument and a debate over whether a representative is more of a fiduciary, where you are given a trust to exercise judgment or whether your job is to figure out what a majority of the individuals in your district care about any one time and represent that. And I would add an asterisk that oftentimes is not what a majority of your constituents feel. It's what a majority of your supporters within your half of the constituency would feel at any one point in time. Obviously, take the former approach that we have. Yes, we're a democracy. We're also a republic. And we try to balance those two philosophies between mob rule on the one hand while protecting things that we feel will be more sacred and recognize in the fact that people change their minds, people are persuadable. What somebody passionately believes one day, we not only should expect that they may change or alter some components of that in time, or not even just believe, but how they view and judge a situation that they may change over time, but that we want to let people change their minds. I think that's one of the dangerous things about I hate the term cancel culture, but it's the best term for this. That there's no accounting for maturation. There's no accounting for evolution. Well, you had a wrong think opinion two decades ago, how dare you, regardless of where you are today. That we should all be so perfect as the",
