    "turn_text": "I think it exists, but it almost exists more at the personal relationship level, right? Like you won't be persuaded by somebody that you can immediately discount or say, well, they're only saying that because X or Y or Z, right? And we have it within the right. It's like, well, I'm not going to listen to anything this person says because they're MAGA or because they're never Trump or, you know, these just baseline rejections that prevent you from being able to say, all right, let's step beyond whatever we're getting our dukes up for, right? And them's fighting words. And it's the exact same thing on the left where, I mean, it's just the, there's always a degree of ad hominem and that it's like, well, I don't need to listen to what this person says because they had some opinion I disagreed with. So that That makes it a bit more challenging on the persuasion side in general, and just the overall balkanization, right? Like you don't have, partially because of the tribal sorting of a lot of issues, you don't have people naturally convening or coalescing around an issue where there are differences of opinion in other places, right? I mean, you're used to have, you know, pro-life and pro-choice Democrats. Or you're sorry, you said pro-life Democrats, you said pro-choice Republicans, right? You used to have, you know, Republicans who advocate for gun control and kind of more hard-line blue-dog Democrats who wanted who were strong proponents of the Second",
