    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "I think there's also certain arrogance that are hubris, that are access to information, and are assumption that we can optimize and control the world. There's a hubris that lends that I think extends into a lot of other realms where we can't just say, we don't know, or, well, our best guess is this. Look at the CDC's guidance around the coronavirus. It's going to be absolute. We can add the touch of our fingertips, access generations of millennia of information and just of untold largesse really flies in the face of a lot of what we should be practicing, which is a degree of humility. Like, I can access this, but is that definitive? Can I know everything? And I think when it comes to politics, and you see this with kind of conspiracy theorists on both sides, and I've had many, many a conversation here where we assume that all knowledge that fits our priors, you know, can stand without questioning. And I tend not to be preachy in a lot of realms because I'm still learning. I'm still grappling. Those who speak most assuredly are oftentimes the people that flame out the fastest because they lose any sense of gut check of saying, well, this is my best operating assumption, but things might change. So I just don't I think our brains are broken on the one hand from this little phone that keeps ringing here. Our brains are utterly broken with that dopamine addiction hit, right? And that assumption that we can access anything and that we have perfect knowledge and that we can optimize and that there's no room for chance, there's no room for serendipity, there's no room for any of the mysteries of life to come in. And I tend to think that those mysteries of life force a degree of humility, force a degree of openness to maybe I'm not right about this, right? We should have our operating assumption, but we should also be questioning that from time to time. And I think there's, you see nothing of that in some of the messianic fervor in some areas of our politics. You see nothing of that humility or that doubt or just No way, I'm going to keep an open mind. I mean, those are incredibly rare characteristics because if you are on the side of right, then the other has to be on the side of wrong. And if you're on the side of right, then you're, why question",
