    "turn_text": "of purpose. A singularity of purpose. And it is who's paying and what they're receiving is pretty well identified. And that's not given too much stretch toward a rev share on the stateside. But then you look at entities that don't function well, and they tend to be of amorphous purpose. They tend to be abstracted from the consequence of their everyday actions. And some of that's going to be inherent. You're not going to have the Department of Homeland Security. Very nebulous, right? I mean, on the committee and we were in this center where it was very big and fancy and talk about how they react to various incidences and as a way how ways are. Where's your product going? Oh, this product is to figure out how we will react internally to a pipeline disruption. Oh, so it's not how the country reacts. No, no, no, it's how we react. Okay. So you have these on the wheels within wheels and then some of these agencies where you can't help but think of the office-based lines like, so what exactly do you do around here? You see that a lot more at the federal government than you do at a state and local where you have to, you have a clearly defined what is your responsibility, who is responsible for this. And if you don't do it, it doesn't get done. We feel a lot more the presence or an absence of a mayor or a governor than frankly we do it for president, right? You could have, you know, if the trash isn't getting picked up, if the police are now on the streets, like you feel that. And to me, there's no better way of trying to reprise and get away from this abstraction and this rudderlessness of our federal government than to return it to that more closely held continuously justify and or continuously have a justification for your actions rather than just going on federal autopilot as we have.",
