    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "you can always claim credit, whether or not that credit deserves to be claimed. You can always be blamed, whether or not that blame deserves to be owned. But so much, I think, of the challenges that are our responsibility, accountability set up in this country, is not geared towards credit where credit is due. It's geared towards blame, avoidance, at all costs. As long as I'm able to wash my hands of it, then I'm fine, not my problem. And yet, I think in our foreign policy, I think that was one of President Obama's failings with the Syrian conflict, is thinking that he could just wash his hands of it and ignore. There's some things that whether you like to or not you're going to be forced to deal with on a domestic context You know, there's a lot more You start to see kind of that ball rolling in the opposite, you know I Don't have really good analogy for this, but if you you're there's a lot is a lot more problematic to try to stop something bad from happening Even if you end up You know causing a better outcome because you can't You'll only be blamed for what did happen, not what didn't. And at the end of the day, I think governments' role should be to be thinking long-term, thinking ahead, rather than just focusing on maybe the idea here and now. but the here and now is all the public is thinking about too, which means it should be the responsibility of your elected officials to be thinking long-term. So we're not heading towards this cliff, right? If you're just, you know, at the local level, you see it with, well, how come our pensions are out of balance? Well, because may or so and so, or councilman, whatever, negotiated a terrible deal that they should be held responsible for and punished. If you're trying to figure out, well, how come our federal debt just went to 30 trillion? Who are you going to blame to that? There's a lot of blame to go around, but none of it's actually going to stick.",
